WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.498 --> 00:00:11.290
JASON DE LORME: Hi.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.110
Welcome to our
session on automating

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.375
the modernization of VM-based
applications to containers.

00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:18.750
My name's Jason
De Lorme, and I'm

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.060
joined by my peer Mike Coleman.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.280
There's three areas
of modernization

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.920
that you can consider
when moving applications

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.240
to the cloud.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.120
The first is infrastructure
modernization.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.570
And when we think about this
on the axis of complexity

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.780
and value, infrastructure
modernization

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.090
is by far the easiest step.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.310
As you graduate
through this scale,

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.920
complexity increases with
changing code and building

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.140
cloud-native applications
all the way up

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.900
to process automation.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.990
Moving and adopting
cloud-native technologies

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:56.460
also requires optimizing
DevOps practices

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.880
and optimizing your
people and processes.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.480
So really to land those
first few workloads,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.030
we're going to talk to you
today about infrastructure

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.770
modernization and some of the
options that you have here.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.380
Often customers go through an
application rationalization

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.960
exercise in order to determine
which applications to move

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.040
to the cloud in which order.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.880
So the first category
of applications,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.210
often called legacy
applications and sometimes

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.920
even referred to as
monolith applications,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.840
that all of the functionality
is into a single binary or one

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.400
complicated application.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.560
And monoliths aren't
always a bad thing.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.230
But when they're VM
based, potentially they

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.610
take a long time to start.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.450
They load logs.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.440
They load cache.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.220
They don't necessarily
lend themselves

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.670
to being able to be replicated
into multiple instances.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.950
We think of these traditionally
as not ready for cloud native.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.880
However, they can be rehosted
into infrastructure VMs,

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.660
so moving from on-prem VMs
into a VM in infrastructure

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.430
as a service, for example.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.410
The other option is
to start thinking

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.990
about maybe decomposing
that monolith,

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.180
and reimagining
it, and refactoring

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.740
to cloud native services.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.500
The second area are what we
might think of as container

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.000
ready.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.730
And this actually might
be bigger than you think.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.145
The options here,
you can clearly

00:02:19.145 --> 00:02:22.400
rehost these applications into
an [INAUDIBLE] based approach

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.220
with VMs, or you
could replatform them

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.490
to something like Kubernetes
and put them in a container,

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.080
and have them orchestrated.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.570
This is really going to be the
focus of our talk here today.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.950
As you think about some
of these other container--

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.905
cloud-compatible,
or cloud-friendly,

00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:40.280
or cloud-native
applications that

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.490
might already be in containers,
those are certainly candidates.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.600
We'll dive into
number two today.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.750
So what are containers?

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.750
If you've been under a rock
for the last five, six,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.470
seven years, then
you've probably

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.000
never heard the term container.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.480
And ultimately they're a method
for packaging up an application

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.370
and all of its dependencies.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.570
The most common phrase you
hear related to containers

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.710
is it works on my machine,
hence it will work in the cloud.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.390
It will work on
somebody else's machine.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.950
And we often get away from some
of those mysterious dependency

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.250
issues or configuration issues
that weren't necessarily

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.880
there when you ran it on your
laptop in dev, for example.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.700
Other phrases that we hear often
associated with containers,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.520
things like being lightweight.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.800
Because it doesn't take
the entire operating

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.670
system-- it's just your
application and all

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.510
the dependencies-- it
becomes more portable.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.010
It's using a standardized
format that can be used.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.660
It helps increase productivity
and ultimately helps

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.890
drive security as well.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.480
So why do we do this?

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.270
Why do we build containers?

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.870
If you think of a traditional
VM-based application,

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.510
often choices were made
to put an application

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:55.820
per VM inside of the
customer's infrastructure

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.210
because of that bleeding
over of dependencies.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.790
Maybe one application
leverages a shared library

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.550
that can get overwritten,
and that can create havoc

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:06.500
for the other application.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.480
We ultimately optimize those
applications for the minimum VM

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.705
constraints for the
operating system--

00:04:13.705 --> 00:04:16.040
a certain amount of memory,
a certain amount of CPU

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.029
and disk--

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:20.370
not based on the application's
requirements itself.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.290
So we often see underutilized
hardware being leveraged

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.100
and also a complicated rollout
process for every single VM,

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.680
because every single
app has its own VM.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.410
If we can move to a
place where applications

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.020
can co-exist and not
step on each other,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.930
and not conflict with
each other in a single VM,

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.430
we can get to a
place where we're

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.130
optimizing the
infrastructure, optimizing

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.550
even the licensing costs
since often the operating

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.020
system is licensed at the
VM and not at the container.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.990
If I can get two applications
into a single VM,

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:56.420
I've halved my licensing
costs for that.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.700
Or I can get to high
availability easier

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.410
with the same licensing
costs because I

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.350
can have two instances of
this application running.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:08.240
Each VM now has
two instances here.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.520
So I mentioned the
container-based approach.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.300
You can use containers for
Windows or Linux machines.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.520
Now, Windows is a
little bit more recent

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.610
when it comes to adopting
containerized strategies.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.670
For that reason there are
a few different behaviors

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.640
versus Linux, which was
built from the beginning

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.047
to support containers.

00:05:30.047 --> 00:05:31.630
There are a handful
of things that you

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:35.050
need to think about when moving
to a containerized environment

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.630
in an orchestrator
like Kubernetes.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:41.170
Specifically, some of
those Windows containers

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.720
don't support privileged pods.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.170
That means those things
like virus-scanning software

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.910
really live at the VM level.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.550
They don't live inside
the container itself.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.700
Those types of services
need to be examined.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.390
Anything that has low-level
network dependencies, where

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:02.410
it's dependent on something
below layer 7 in the networking

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.900
stack, for example,
you don't necessarily

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.250
want to depend on the IP
address of another machine

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.150
or joining a cluster where
there's a heartbeat in between

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.900
and they need to know
a static IP address.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:17.770
Can be a little problematic
when you think about IPs.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.860
And we can talk about
ways of addressing that.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.020
If there's low-level
GPU or TPU support,

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.690
this isn't available in
a Windows container yet.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:29.020
Cloud-native features like
Istio to create a service mesh

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.950
in order to help with traffic
management and service

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:33.760
discovery.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.850
This isn't supported in
Windows containers just yet,

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:40.000
nor is being able to use
something like CloudRun

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.300
or Knative, the open source
version where you can steal

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.130
the zero in a
serverless environment--

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:49.640
not quite ready for
Windows containers.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.220
So let's talk about
what is ready.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.380
And what are the ideal
container candidates?

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.680
So first off, I would look at
your web applications, your web

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.260
and logic tiers, things
that-- maybe they're

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:04.210
written in Python, PHP,
Java, ASP.NET, for example,

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.400
those things hosted in IIS.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.040
Those are great
candidates potentially

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.070
to run inside of a container
in a container-orchestrated

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.380
environment like Kubernetes.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:18.430
Another area to look at is batch
jobs or console applications

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.130
that may be running
spiky workloads,

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:24.520
things that run maybe
every day at 6:00 PM

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.340
or things that run and
listen to queues that don't

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.630
have any kind of GUI to them.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.590
They're an ideal candidate.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.542
Even Windows Services,
those are applications

00:07:35.542 --> 00:07:37.750
that are registered with
the Windows operating system

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.920
to begin at startup,
for example.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.110
They may even log to the
Windows application event log.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.230
All these things can be
containerized and run

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.900
in a container orchestrator.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.240
Now let me jump to the lower
right-hand side of this

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.850
and talk about those
container workloads that

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:56.223
are potentially unsuitable.

00:07:56.223 --> 00:07:57.640
And I'm going to
start right away.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.220
I mentioned GUI, the
graphical user interface.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:02.620
Anything that has
a GUI is not going

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:05.260
to be a good candidate
for a container.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:09.100
You can't run a remote desktop
protocol session, for example,

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.130
into a container.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.060
So virtualized desktop
infrastructure, VDI,

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.790
for example, is not
a fit for containers.

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:19.360
If the application
you're using is primarily

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.510
centered around user interaction
through a Win32 console,

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:24.190
for example, that
is not something

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:26.570
that's going to run
inside of a container.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.450
Now, there's a
gray area of things

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:31.750
that you may want to investigate
further and use a fit

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.630
assessment tool, for example,
to determine whether it's

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:37.960
appropriate for running in a
container-- things on the Linux

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.159
side where there's a GPU or
TPU dependency, areas where

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:45.040
the container requires
elevated privileges.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.300
Another big category here
is commercial off-the-shelf

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.660
software, or COTS.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:54.130
Now, most ISVs have come
out with newer versions

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.740
of their applications that
are containerized already.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.410
So it's often a good fit to
think about maybe a version

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.753
upgrade that will take you to
their container-based approach.

00:09:02.753 --> 00:09:04.420
However, there are a
lot of applications

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:06.850
where maybe the vendor
is no longer in business,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:08.890
or maybe it's critical
to the business,

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:12.795
but nobody really knows what
the application was written in.

00:09:12.795 --> 00:09:13.920
You just have the binaries.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.628
You don't have access
to the source code.

00:09:15.628 --> 00:09:17.050
They could be a fit.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.810
However, we should run
an assessment tool here

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.600
in order to determine whether
or not it will work well

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.590
in a containerized environment.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.040
And with that I'd like to
introduce my peer Mike Coleman,

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.540
who can talk to you
about a solution

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.520
that we have at Google
to help you with this.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.543
Mike?

00:09:31.543 --> 00:09:32.710
MIKE COLEMAN: Thanks, Jason.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.668
I want to talk to you
today about a tool called

00:09:34.668 --> 00:09:36.400
Migrate for Anthos and GKE.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.620
We built this tool
to help automate

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:40.750
the migration of virtual
machine-based workloads

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:46.540
from environments like Amazon,
AWS, VMWare, Azure, and even

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.100
Google Cloud Compute Engine.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.422
This tool automatically
inspects those container--

00:09:50.422 --> 00:09:52.630
those virtual machines--
excuse me-- and convert them

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:55.510
to containers where then you can
start taking advantage of some

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.850
of the benefits that Jason
mentioned previously, things

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:01.720
like improved resource
utilization, unified logging

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:05.030
and monitoring, and then
potentially day 2 operations.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.485
So it's one thing to
talk about a tool.

00:10:07.485 --> 00:10:09.110
It's another thing
to actually show it.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.230
So I want to do a
quick demo where

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:13.240
I'm going to take
you through a payment

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.860
service-based virtual
machine migration.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.497
So I have an online
boutique, an online shopping,

00:10:18.497 --> 00:10:21.080
and a bunch of the services are
already running in containers.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.140
So think of the front
end, the shopping

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:24.790
cart, some of those services.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.100
They call back to
a payment service

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:29.950
that's running in a virtual
machine on Google Cloud.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:33.250
We're going to migrate
that VM into a container

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.410
and integrate it with the
rest of the application.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.970
We'll start with
the fit assessment.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.040
As Jason mentioned, it's
critical to understand

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.117
how well your VM-based workload
can run in a container.

00:10:42.117 --> 00:10:44.200
From there we're going to
create a migration plan.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:45.880
We'll make a few tweaks to that.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.973
We'll run the migration.

00:10:46.973 --> 00:10:48.640
We'll get a set of
deployment artifacts.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.000
We'll deploy those.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.410
And then finally, we'll
edit the service discovery

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:56.050
so that it's not looking for
the virtual machine, but rather

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.980
the payment service running
as a Kubernetes service.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.650
This is the VM that we're
going to migrate today

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:05.590
into a container
running on Kubernetes.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:06.700
So let's go ahead.

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.320
I'm going to go up here and
close this down because we

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:09.262
don't need it anymore.

00:11:09.262 --> 00:11:11.720
And let's take a look at this
application actually running.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.210
So if I come over
here and I click

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.130
on one of the products,
the antique camera,

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.470
I click Add to Cart,
and then I click

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:20.740
Place Order, everything's
working as expected.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.050
That order was processed
using the payment service

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.260
on the virtual machine.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.885
So the first thing we need to do
to get this all working though

00:11:26.885 --> 00:11:28.690
is to stop the
payment service VM.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:32.200
A virtual machine can't be
migrated until it's stopped.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.600
With the VM stopped, let's go
ahead and clear the screen,

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.040
and close out of Cloud Shell.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.560
Now let's take a look at the
migration fit assessment.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.840
I've created a report
by running an agent

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:44.320
on the virtual machine, and it's
stored here in this JSON file.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.150
I can upload this in
the Cloud Console,

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:48.608
and it's going to give me an
indication that my application

00:11:48.608 --> 00:11:49.900
is a good fit for migration.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.910
If I want to drill down,
I can click on the name

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:53.470
and get a list of all
the different things that

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:54.340
were inspected.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.060
We know that we're
good to go here,

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.977
so why don't we go ahead
and close out of this

00:11:57.977 --> 00:11:59.373
and actually start
the migration?

00:11:59.373 --> 00:12:00.790
I'm going to go
over here, and I'm

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.582
going to click on
Migrations, and I'm going

00:12:02.582 --> 00:12:04.180
to click Create Migration.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:09.060
I'm going to give
my migration a name.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.120
I'm going to call it
online boutique migration.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:12.770
We'll choose our source.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.930
The instance name was
payment service VM.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.680
And we're going to
just do the image.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.980
So let's create the migration.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:20.840
In the interest of
time I've sped this up

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.090
so we can move on to our
next step, which is to review

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:24.257
and edit the migration plan.

00:12:24.257 --> 00:12:26.382
So the migration plan comes
up, and the first thing

00:12:26.382 --> 00:12:27.770
I want to do is
add an annotation

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:30.800
to tell Migrate for GKE to
create an image that can

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.660
run on an autopilot cluster.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.960
I also want to go and adjust the
image name and the Kubernetes

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:39.020
service name and deployment
name to payment service instead

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.270
of payment service VMs since
we're not running in a VM

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:42.600
any longer.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.940
So this takes a few minutes.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.257
I've sped the video up.

00:12:45.257 --> 00:12:46.715
I'll rejoin you
when it's finished.

00:12:49.705 --> 00:12:51.580
With the process completed,
let's take a look

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.123
at the artifacts
that were created.

00:12:53.123 --> 00:12:54.790
So I've come down
here under Next Steps.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.860
I click Options and
Review Artifacts.

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:59.235
And that will bring up a list
of what was created for us.

00:12:59.235 --> 00:13:00.860
The first thing you'll
see are a couple

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:02.860
of Docker images one
that will actually deploy

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:05.180
and one we can use
for day 2 operations.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.930
We also get a Dockerfile and
a Kubernetes deployment spec.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.000
So if I open up
Cloud Shell, you'll

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:11.963
see that I've already
downloaded these files.

00:13:11.963 --> 00:13:12.880
So I'll do an LS here.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.050
You can see all the files.

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:17.140
Now, this Kubernetes
deployment_spec.yaml is going

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:18.940
to create a new
Kubernetes deployment,

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.100
and it's going to create
a new Kubernetes service

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.850
for our migrated workloads.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.190
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:13:26.498 --> 00:13:28.040
Let's make sure
everything's started.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:29.415
We'll take a look
at the service.

00:13:29.415 --> 00:13:30.640
And there it is running.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.140
And let's take a
look at the pod.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:33.860
And it's running as well.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.760
So what we're going to do now
is check on our application.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.860
Now, actually, I
expect this to fail.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.150
So let's come in here.

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:41.710
Let's click on the typewriter.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:44.927
And let's go ahead and add that
to our cart, and check out.

00:13:44.927 --> 00:13:45.760
So it's in our cart.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:46.600
Place order.

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.730
Now, what happened
is it's trying

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:52.570
to reach the payment service
on the DNS name of the VM.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.880
So what we need
to do is actually

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.140
modify the deployment
spec for our application.

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.515
And we need to change it from
looking at the internal DNS

00:13:59.515 --> 00:14:01.820
name of the VM to the
Kubernetes service name.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.280
So that's payment service.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:06.040
So we'll chop off the DNS
name here and just leave

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.090
payment service.

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:08.590
We'll save that out.

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:12.250
And then we can go back in and
rerun that deployment YAML.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:14.240
And that's going to
reprovision our services.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:15.490
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:19.120
We'll do a kubectl apply and
apply that updated boutique

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:20.530
deployment.yaml.

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.577
It'll reconfigure the services.

00:14:22.577 --> 00:14:24.410
So let's go ahead and
clear the screen here.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:25.480
Let's look at the pods.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:27.910
It looks like the checkout
service has been reconfigured

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:28.910
and is running.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.680
So if I come in here and
I click on the typewriter,

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.800
and I add it to cart,
and I place order,

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.480
everything is
working as expected.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:39.580
We've successfully migrated
our payment service

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.710
from a virtual machine
into a container

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:46.250
running on a GKE autopilot
cluster using Migrate for GKE.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:47.180
So that's the demo.

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:48.050
And demos are great.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:49.180
They give you a feel
for the product,

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.055
and you can see some of
the things in action.

00:14:51.055 --> 00:14:53.450
But it's important also to
look at how the tool's used

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.330
in the real world.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.690
So I want to start by
discussing a customer example.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:57.890
This is The Telegraph.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.530
They're a content management,
content publication service

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:01.640
over in the UK.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.312
And they had a legacy
content management system

00:15:04.312 --> 00:15:05.270
that they were running.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:06.980
It was super important
to their business,

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:08.720
but it was a little
difficult for them

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.980
to manually migrate
it into containers.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.720
So they used Migrate for Anthos
to do that work for them--

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:16.580
vastly simplified the process.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.747
And they're already
realizing savings,

00:15:18.747 --> 00:15:21.080
not just on the infrastructure
as Jason mentioned before

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.490
with the bin
packing and whatnot,

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:27.950
but also in the day-to-day
operation of that CMS.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:28.928
So that's one customer.

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:29.720
That's one example.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:30.980
Now, what about yourself?

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.230
Now, if you're going
to look at migrating,

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.990
there's some things I
want you to consider.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.370
These are six items.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:40.130
They're not insurmountable by
any stretch of the imagination.

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:42.835
They're really presented
here just as things for you

00:15:42.835 --> 00:15:43.460
to be aware of.

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:45.810
And the first we
saw in the demo.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.820
We were using VM-based
DNS hostname discovery.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.310
So we had the DNS name
of that payment service.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.560
We converted that to
a Kubernetes service.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.130
So we let Kubernetes take care
of that service discovery using

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.100
its own internal DNS structure.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.290
Along those same lines,
if you have host files

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:06.800
that you've customized
and you've got hostnames,

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.770
you can use host aliases in the
pod spec to take care of that.

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:12.680
So that's another
simple migration.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:14.820
Again, looking at the
pod YAML, a couple

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.430
other things we could take
care of are file shares.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:18.540
So file shares are great.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:19.250
You can use them.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.350
We just don't automatically
migrate them for you.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:26.510
You're going to need to define
NFS persistent volumes inside

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:29.433
of the pod spec to
take care of those.

00:16:29.433 --> 00:16:31.100
And the same with
environment variables.

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.050
You may be using an
environment variable

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:36.570
for the URI for your
database, for instance.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.272
You can actually define that
same environment variable

00:16:39.272 --> 00:16:40.730
in the pod spec,
and then you don't

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.800
need to change any other
applications because you're

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.717
getting the same information
you're expecting.

00:16:44.717 --> 00:16:47.750
So find the environment
variable in the pod YAML,

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:49.490
and that'll take care of that.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:51.890
Now, I want to say something--

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:53.480
and I might say it
kind of forcefully

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:55.040
because I really believe it--

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:57.470
just because you're migrating
something to a container

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:00.590
doesn't mean that you don't
have to understand how it works

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:01.850
and how it runs.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:04.849
And matter of fact, if you want
to have a successful migration,

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:07.609
it's critical that you
understand what you have

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:08.900
and how it works.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.085
When you go in and you
look at a migration,

00:17:11.085 --> 00:17:13.460
you should be looking at the
services and the agents that

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:16.670
are running in that VM and
make a conscious decision on

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.190
whether or not you need
them in your container.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.890
For instance, you
may have an agent

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:24.111
running that's doing
logging and monitoring.

00:17:24.111 --> 00:17:25.819
And we'll talk about
that in a little bit

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:28.855
in the next bulletpoint, but you
probably want to disable that.

00:17:28.855 --> 00:17:30.230
You also want to
make sure you're

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:32.010
running at the right run level.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:33.740
So go through, audit
those services.

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:36.080
Disable the ones you don't need.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.390
That will help minimize
container image size,

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.860
and improve performance,
and reduce complexity.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.620
So this is a really
important one

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:43.470
that I want you to go through.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.095
The other thing that's
not specifically

00:17:45.095 --> 00:17:48.710
called out here along the same
vein, file system structure.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:51.560
Are there directories
and files that you don't

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.855
need in the resulting image?

00:17:53.855 --> 00:17:56.690
You can tell Migrate
for Anthos and Migrate

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.720
for GKE to not include certain
directories, certain files.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.270
Again, smaller image
size, better performance.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:06.320
And then finally--
I alluded to it--

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:07.640
application logging.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:10.850
We integrate Migrate for
Anthos and Migrate for GKE

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:14.360
with the cloud, Google
Cloud metrics, logging,

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:15.530
and monitoring services.

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.880
So you don't actually
need to have those agents.

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.880
You don't need to do that
work in the container.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.920
We'll just pick that
stuff up for you,

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.650
and you can monitor
it in Cloud Console

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.228
right there within Google Cloud.

00:18:27.228 --> 00:18:28.770
So that's what we
have for you today.

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.853
I really want to thank you
for tuning in on behalf

00:18:30.853 --> 00:18:31.723
of myself and Jason.

00:18:31.723 --> 00:18:33.890
Why don't you go out, have
a great rest of your day,

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:37.540
and look at migrating some
of those VM workloads?

